/**
 * Fourier feature projection of a scalar — the continuous-value embedding a diffusion model uses
 * to turn a timestep (or any scalar conditioner) into a bank of sinusoids the transformer can read.
 * Each input value is projected onto a frequency ladder and emitted as the cosine of every
 * frequency followed by the sine of every frequency:
 *
 *   fourier_features(t) = [ cos(2π · t · f_0) ... cos(2π · t · f_{half-1}),
 *                           sin(2π · t · f_0) ... sin(2π · t · f_{half-1}) ]
 *
 * This file replaces the Java producer assembly that used to back the block form of
 * DiffusionTransformerFeatures.fourierFeatures / expoFourierFeatures (a single layer wrapping
 * concat(cos(2π · x · frequenciesᵀ), sin(...))). Those methods now only bind the frequency matrix
 * and load this asset, so there is one block-level definition of the structure and it is here. The
 * learned variant binds a trained [half, in] weight; the deterministic (expo) variant binds a fixed
 * geometric frequency ladder. The producer-level overloads that compose into a larger producer graph
 * (NumberConditioner) legitimately stay in Java, exactly as siluMlp keeps a producer form alongside
 * silu_mlp.pdsl.
 *
 * Data flow of one forward pass over a conditioning vector [batch, in]:
 *
 *   [batch, in] conditioning values
 *     dense(frequencies)                                 f_0 = values · frequenciesᵀ   [batch, half]
 *     scale(2π)                                          f = 2π · f_0                   [batch, half]
 *     concat_blocks( cos, sin )                          [cos-plane | sin-plane]        [2·batch·half]
 *     reshape([2, batch, half])                          recover the two planes         [2, batch, half]
 *     permute(1, 0, 2)                                   interleave per row             [batch, 2, half]
 *     reshape([batch, 2·half])                           [cos_b | sin_b] per row        [batch, 2·half]
 *
 * `frequencies` is the projection matrix of shape [half, in]; `half` is the number of frequencies
 * (the output width is 2·half) and `batch` the batch dimension.
 *
 * Stage 1 dense(frequencies) is the bias-less projection onto the frequency ladder. Stage 2 scales
 * by 2π so the projection is a phase in radians. Stage 3 takes the cosine and the sine of that phase:
 * concat_blocks lays them out flat as [all cosines | all sines] (a [2, batch, half] block in
 * row-major order), so the transpose of stages 4–6 interleaves the two planes into the [batch, 2·half]
 * layout where each row carries its own [cos | sin] pair — the cosines first, then the sines.
 */
layer fourier_features(batch: int, half: int, frequencies: weight) {
    dense(frequencies)
    scale(2 * 3.141592653589793)
    concat_blocks(
        { cos() },
        { sin() }
    )
    reshape([2, batch, half])
    permute(1, 0, 2)
    reshape([batch, 2 * half])
}
